{\*\generator Msftedit 5.41.15.1507;}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\b\f0\fs40 PhotoComposer\cf0\f1\fs28 , instruction of use for version 1.00E\par
\par
\b0\fs20 The initial purpose of this program is to convert any picture or photography to a postcard, that can be printed or sent as e-mail attachment through the internet.\par
\par
However while using \b PhotoComposer\b0  you will soon discover that it can do more, much more... \par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Main features:\fs20\line\b0\par
\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li360 Each graphic object, image or text, uses a \b distinct layer\b0 .\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}The overlapping (\b z-order\b0 ) of the layers can range from bottom to top.\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Variable opacity\b0  adjustable for each of the layer.\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Full range \b rotation on 360\'b0\b0  for each of the layer.\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Full customization of the \b TrueType text font\b0  being used.\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Distinct \b zooming magnification\b0  for each of the layer.\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}High quality display\b0  to suppress the "stair case" effect while zooming.\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Dedicated \b border frame\b0  library (in the "\b Borders\b0 " sub-folder). \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Dedicated \b PNG icon\b0  library (in the "\b Icons\b0 " sub-folder).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Drag & drop\b0  between Windows Explorer and the \b PhotoComposer\b0  form.\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Supported graphic format: \b bmp, gif, jpg, jpeg, png, tif, tiff\b0 .\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Outlook Express\b0  compatibility to send the resulting image as an attachment.\par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Full compatibility with Windows \b VISTA\b0  and \b XP\b0 .\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}PhotoComposer\b0  is powered by \cf1\b GDImage\cf2\b0 .\par
\pard\par
\par
\cf0\b\fs24 How to use it:\b0\fs20\line\line\b 1 - Select a new background photography:\b0\par
Use the control panel "\cf1\b Select background picture\cf0\b0 " button to pick one, the selected picture will be shown, while all other pictures laying into the same folder are loaded into a hidden playlist. \par
\line You can also use "\cf1\b drag & drop\cf0\b0 " from the Windows Explorer to select a new background.\par
\par
For further details see the "\cf1\b Play loop mode\cf0\b0 " button section.\par
\par
\b 2 - Add picture:\b0\par
Use the control panel "\cf1\b Add picture\cf0\b0 " button, the selected picture will be shown hover the existing background in half the size of the view port, it is up to you to move it at another location using the size ("zooming" trackbar), rotation (see "Perfom full 360\'b0 rotation"), opacity ("opacity level" trackbar) of your choice.\par
\par
\b 3 - Add annotation:\b0\par
Use the control panel "\cf1\b Add annotation\cf0\b0 " button, the text editor dialog pops up to let you enter the annotation, select the font, size, color and shadow (3D) effect. Once done, the new annotation is shown centered hover all existing layers, it is up to you to move it at another location using the size ("zooming" trackbar), rotation (see "Full 360\'b0 range rotation"), opacity ("opacity level" trackbar) of your choice.\par
\par
\b 4 - Add icon:\b0\par
Use the control panel "\cf1\b Add icon\cf0\b0 " button, the selected icon will be shown centered on the view port, it is up to you to move it at another location using the size ("zooming" trackbar), rotation (see "Full 360\'b0 range rotation"), opacity ("opacity level" trackbar) of your choice\par
\par
\b 5 - Add transparent picture:\par
\b0 Use the control panel "\cf1\b Add picture using variable opacity\cf0\b0 " button to pick one, then a special filter is applied to the selected picture to create the variable opacity effect, based on each of the pixel luminous intensity, ranging from full back (transparent mode) to full white (opaque mode) like in shade of gray.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Contextual menus:\b0\fs20\par
\par
To popup contextual menu, clic the right mouse button on any graphic object (image, annotation, text, background) the content of the menu matches the type of the graphic object, to let you \cf1\b select a specific command\cf0\b0 :\par
\par
\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-284\li284\b Tex edit \b0 (to edit the text itself and change all font parameters)\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Flip horizontaly\b0\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Flip verticaly\b0\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Rotate 90\'b0 counterclock\b0\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Rotate 90\'b0 clockwise\b0\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Top\b0  (of the z-order).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Up\b0  (of the z-order).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Down\b0  (of the z-order).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Bottom\b0  (of the z-order).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Miniature 128x128\b0  (convert to PNG icon preserving the alpha channel).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Miniature 256x256\b0  (convert to PNG icon preserving the alpha channel).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Clone\b0  (duplicate an object).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Lock move\b0  (protect from unwanted move).\par
\b{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Delete\b0  (remove the object).\par
\pard\par
\par
\b\fs24 Moving the PhotoComposer window form hover the desktop:\b0\par
\fs20\par
In \b PhotoComposer\b0  the border frame acts like a standard form caption to let you drag the window to another desktop location.\line\par
You can use either the \cf1\b border frame\cf0\b0  or the \cf1\b bottom control panel\cf0\b0 , to drag the form hover the Windows desktop.\par
\par
\b\fs24\par
To select a border frame:\par
\b0\fs20\par
Clic on button "\cf1\b next frame\cf0\b0 " or "\cf1\b previous frame\cf0\b0 " to select one.\par
Note: you can hide/show the border at any time using the "\cf1\b border frame\cf0\b0 " button.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Moving a graphic object:\b0\fs20\par
\par
When the mouse hover a graphic object that can be moved, the cursor's shape changes from the default arrow shape to a four direction cross.\par
\par
Before you can move or perform any action on a graphic object, it must have first the focus, thus clic the left mouse button on the one you want to select. Then you can drag it to another location while holding down the left mouse button. \par
\par
You can also \cf1\b move it very precisely\cf0\b0 , pixel per pixel, using the \cf1\b keyboard arrow keys\cf0\b0 . To speed up the moving, hold down either the left SHIFT key, the CTRL key, or a combination of SHIFT+CTRL, while you use any of the arrow key.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Moving several graphic objects at once:\b0\fs20\par
\par
Before you can move several objects at once, you must first select them. To do it, \cf1\b hold down the left "CTRL" key\cf0\b0 , then select the objects you want with the \cf1\b left mouse button\cf0\b0 . Once done, choose any of the selected object and drag it to its new location, and this will also move the other selected objects to their respective location. To remove the selection, double clic either on the background or the frame border.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Perfom full 360\'b0 rotation:\b0\fs20\par
\par
You can rotate any of the image, PNG icon, background, and text annotation at any angle within the 0-360\'b0 range, by step of 1 degree, either clockwise or counter-clockwise.\par
\par
To do it, put the focus on the object you want to rotate, then hold down the \cf1\b left SHIFT key\cf0\b0 , while using the \cf1\b mouse wheel\cf0\b0 .\par
\par
Note: By design, you can't perform full rotation nor direct zooming of the "Title" and "Signature", because they are not plain annotation but "standard" GDImage text font. \par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Zooming:\b0\fs20\par
\par
To enlarge or reduce the size of either background, image or annotation, first clic on the object to select it (focus), then \cf1\b use the zooming trackbar\cf0\b0  from the control panel.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Croping the background:\b0\fs20\par
\par
Just move the image background inside of the frame and adjust the numerical zoom to enlarge or reduce the size.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Customizing Title or Signature:\par
\b0\fs20\par
Select the "\cf1\b Edit\cf0\b0 " option from the contextual menu to popup the dialog text font editor. You can then select a new True Type font, change its size, color, translucency and edit the text content.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 Deleting the image background:\par
\b0\par
\fs20 Use the control panel "\cf1\b Send to tray\cf0\b0 " button, the picture is sent directly to tray without further confirmation.\par
\par
\par
\fs24 "\b Play loop mode" button:\b0\fs20\par
\par
Once a background has been selected, all other images laying into the same folder are kept into a hidden \b PhotoComposer\b0 's \cf1\b playlist\cf0\b0 . When you press the "\cf1\b Play loop mode\cf0\b0 " button, then you start changing the background in sequence like in a slide show. You can use the "\cf1\b Slide show delay\cf0\b0 " trackbar to adjust the speed transition between pictures, ranging from 0 to 8 seconds (the default is 4 seconds). While playing the show you can change the slide order using "\cf1\b Previous background\cf0\b0 " or "\cf1\b Next background\cf0\b0 ". To stop the show press the "\cf1\b Pause\cf0\b0 " button.\par
\par
Note: As soon as a background has been selected you can use the "\cf1\b Previous background\cf0\b0 " and "\cf1\b Next background\cf0\b0 " buttons to navigate the hidden list, there is no need to be first in "\cf1\b Play loop mode\cf0\b0 ". \par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Save PhotoComposition" button:\par
\b0\fs20\par
You will \cf1\b save to disk\cf0\b0 , in WYSIWYG mode, your photo composition (postcard) into the folder of your choice, and then you can do what ever you want with it :-)\par
\par
\par
\pard\tx284\b\fs24 "Print" button:\par
\b0\par
\pard\fs20 The photo composition (postcard) is \cf1\b sent to the printer\cf0\b0  in WYSIWYG mode.\par
\par
Note: For best result, use a 4 x 6 inches format (101.6 x 152.4 millimeters).\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Border frame" button:\b0\par
\fs20\par
Use it to \cf1\b hide or show \cf0\b0 the frame border.\par
\par
Note: When the frame border is hidden, you must use the control panel if you want to move the \b PhotoComposer\b0  form hover the Windows desktop to a new location.\par
\par
Remark: If you double clic onto the frame border while visible, then you iconize the \b PhotoComposer\b0  application.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Delimit crop section" button:\fs20\par
\b0\par
When you use it, the shape of the cursor changes to let you know that the "\cf1\b crop mode\cf0\b0 " is on. \par
\par
Clic the left mouse button to anchor the top left corner of the delimited section and hold it down while moving the mouse to resize the rectangular frame. \par
\par
To resize the delimited section move the mouse hover the frame and drag it to its new location.\par
\par
Once done, use a mouse double clic to popup the contextual menu, then choose "\cf1\b Select\cf0\b0 " to validate the capture or "\cf1\b Cancel\cf0\b0 " if you want to delimit another section. When "Select" is being used you ear a small beep to inform you that the \cf1\b delimited section\cf0\b0  has been \cf1\b sent to the Windows clipboard\cf0\b0 .\par
\par
To leave the "crop mode" and restore the default mouse cursor, double clic the left mouse button on the background or the frame border.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Send postcard" button:\b0\fs20\par
This is to send the postcard as an \cf1\b e-mail attachment\cf0\b0  using \cf1\b Outlook Express\cf0\b0 .\par
\par
Note: If the \cf1\b stamp\cf0\b0  is shown already, then you can either \cf1\b double clic\cf0\b0  on it, to \cf1\b send the postcard\cf0\b0 .\par
\par
Once Outlook Express is open, then you can select one or several addressee. The object and the text of the message are pre-fulfilled and the postcard itself is already attached to the mail.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Switch Language" button:\b0\fs20\par
The small flag shown on the bottom left corner allows you to switch between English and French. This affects not only the texts, the tooltips and the user help, but also the image of the stamp being used.\par
\par
\pard\tx284\b\par
\pard\fs24 "Close" button:\b0\fs20\par
This is to leave the application, it is up to you to \cf1\b save first your PhotoComposition\cf0\b0  if you want to keep it.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Save as a PCS file" button:\b0\fs20\par
\par
\b PhotoComposer\b0  allows you to create sophisticated presentations, using many images, texts, icons, with individual setups for transparency, rotation, zooming etc. that could represent a fairly amount of work.\par
\par
Thus to save you the pain to rebuild a specific presentation each time you need it, you can save your work as a PCS script files (\cf3\b P\cf0\b0 hoto\cf3\b C\cf0\b0 omposer \cf3\b S\cf0\b0 cript). All the setups for each of the objects and the \b disk location\b0  of each image are stored in this file.\par
But you must take care of this: \cf3\b do not delete, rename, move the pictures being used in a script\cf0\b0 , or they won't be shown (not found) on further use of the script.\par
\par
\cf1\b Note: There is no automatic PCS backup, it is up to you to do it regularly. \line\cf0\b0\par
The default name being used to create a\b  PCS file\b0  is the \b title of the current composition\b0 , PCS files are saved in the application's "\cf1\b Script\cf0\b0 " sub-folder.\par
\par
\par
\b\fs24 "Load a PCS file" button:\b0\fs20\par
\par
Open an existing PCS file, to restore a specific composition for the purpose of display or to change any of the object setup.\par
\par
\b Warning:\b0\par
All objects being used in the current work will be lost in favor of those from the script, \cf1\b it is your responsability to save your work first!\par
\cf0\b0\par
\par
\cf3\b\fs36 Goodies:\cf0\fs24\par
\b0\fs20\par
You can add your own PNG icons to use them with \b PhotoComposer\b0 .\par
To do it, just copy them into the "\cf3\b Icons\cf0\b0 " sub-folder located into the directory that have been used to install \b PhotoComposer\b0 .\par
\par
You will find on the Internet many places to download PNG icons, here are a few of them:\par
\cf1\ul\f2 http://www.crystalxp.net\cf0\ulnone\fs24\par
\cf1\ul\f1\fs20 http://www.yellowicon.com\par
http://www.customxp.net\cf0\ulnone\par
\par
The \cf1\b frame borders\cf0\b0  are 32-bit PNG files using ARGB colors with alpha channel. They are stored into the "\cf3\b Borders\cf0\b0 " sub-folder located into the directory that have been used to install \b PhotoComposer\b0 .\par
\par
All frame borders must have exactly a \b fixed size\b0  of \cf3\b 1024 x 731\cf2\b0  pixels.\par
\par
If you want you can edit those existing, or add new one that you design yourself using a graphic editor handling the alpha channel (ex: "The Gimp" or "PhotoShop").\par
\par
As a complement to the alpha channel being used in PNG files, you can also use any graphic picture using the \cf4\b magenta\cf2  \b0 color RGB(255,0,255) that will be used by \b PhotoComposer\b0  to simulate the transparent region.\par
\par
